"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §§{#} and {#} (orbits and Kepler’s laws) and §{#} (center of mass), and Vol. {#}, §{#} (blackbody radiation and the Stefan–Boltzmann law). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Star and planet, with its own setups; Two stars, with Sun and red dwarf; Twin stars; Hot star, cool giant; Partial eclipses; Eccentric pair; and a Mystery system with its own button. This worksheet uses <strong>Two stars</strong>.",
"Two stars orbit their center of mass. The top view (or 3D) shows the orbit, what the telescope sees shows the two disks as they pass in front of each other, and below are the light curve and both stars’ radial velocities. Drag a star or either graph to move in time.",
"The light curve can show the full orbit, or zoom on the eclipse of star {#} (star {#} in front) or on the eclipse of star {#} (star {#} behind). Time is counted from the middle of the eclipse of star {#}.",
"The sliders set each star’s mass, radius and temperature, the pair’s velocity γ along our line of sight, and the orbit. Noise adds random scatter to every measurement.",
