"Weigh and measure two stars: find their masses from their velocities, their radii from the timing of the eclipses, and the second star’s temperature from the depth of an eclipse.",
"<strong>Velocities and masses.</strong> Both stars orbit the center of mass, so \\(M_1 v_1 = -M_2 v_2\\) for their velocities relative to it, and the velocity semi amplitudes obey \\(K_1/K_2 = M_2/M_1\\). The measured velocities also include the pair’s own velocity γ. For a circular orbit seen edge on (\\(i = 90°\\)), the stars’ relative speed is \\(v = K_1 + K_2 = 2\\pi a/P\\), and Kepler’s third law \\(G(M_1 + M_2)P^2 = 4\\pi^2 a^3\\) gives",
"\\[M_1 + M_2 = \\frac{P(K_1 + K_2)^3}{2\\pi G}.\\]",
"If the orbit is tilted, the velocities give \\(M\\sin^3 i\\); eclipses happen only when \\(i\\) is close to {#}°, which is why eclipsing binaries give true masses.",
"<strong>Radii from the contacts.</strong> An eclipse has four contacts: first (the disks touch), second (the smaller disk is entirely in front of or behind the larger), third and fourth. For a central eclipse the smaller star crosses in a nearly straight line at the speed \\(v\\), so",
"\\[R_{\\text{large}} + R_{\\text{small}} = \\tfrac12 v\\,(t_4 - t_1),\\qquad R_{\\text{large}} - R_{\\text{small}} = \\tfrac12 v\\,(t_3 - t_2).\\]",
"<strong>Light and temperature.</strong> A star’s luminosity is \\(L = 4\\pi R^2\\sigma T^4\\). When the smaller star is completely hidden (a total eclipse), the brightness left is the larger star’s share of the light, \\(f = L_1/(L_1 + L_2)\\). When equal areas are hidden, the eclipse depths are in the ratio of the surface brightnesses, \\(T^4\\).",
"\\[G = 6.674\\times10^{-11}\\ \\text{N m}^2/\\text{kg}^2,\\quad M_\\odot = 1.988\\times10^{30}\\ \\text{kg},\\quad R_\\odot = 6.957\\times10^{8}\\ \\text{m}.\\]",
"Star {#} swings by \\(K_1 = 45\\) km/s and star {#} by \\(K_2 = 150\\) km/s. Which star is heavier, and what is \\(M_2/M_1\\)?",
